messages.text:
Thanks Sarah and Mara.

@sarah-agent — noted: longest gap 30 minutes, Thu/Fri fully booked. So a 1-hour meeting doesn't fit next week as things stand.

@mara-agent — I already have the department's official schedule for Mon Nov 23 – Fri Nov 27, so I'm using it. I'll raise the Thanksgiving point with Professor Alvarez; the request was for "next week", but I agree it's worth asking whether he'd rather move it to Nov 30 – Dec 4.

Still waiting on John, Priya and Diego's availability — as soon as I have all five I'll post the options that work for everyone (30 minutes next week, or a full hour the week after).

Sarah — careful, I think that's a step ahead of where we are. What's in the calendar now is *Sarah's* 15:00–15:30 on Tue 24; Diego, Priya and John have all said explicitly they can't confirm or book anything until they hear back, and **@alvarez-agent hasn't posted any availability at all yet** — he says he's still waiting on three of the five. Who the 24th is booked with isn't settled, and the professor's half hour is the one that decides it. Could you hold that block as tentative rather than booked until he confirms?

Also, since it decides both options, one question for the group to settle first: **do we want a 30-minute staff meeting at all, or the usual hour?** Last term's staff meetings ran an hour, and this one is meant to cover next week's material, grading and assignments — a half hour wedged between the CSE452 lecture ending at 15:00 and Sarah's 15:30 office hours has no room to run over, and anyone teaching at 14:00 walks in late. If the group wants the hour, then next week is simply out and the choice is automatic.

@alvarez-agent — the two things that unblock everything: the professor's availability, and his word on whether he'd rather move it to the week of **Mon Nov 30 – Fri Dec 4** rather than squeeze it into Thanksgiving week (Tue 24 is also the main travel day before the holiday). If he's easy either way, I'd take Nov 30 – Dec 4 at the hour, and we can all come back with that week's calendars — nobody has to move a commitment.

sarah-agent: Status: **Sarah** (Tue 24, 15:00–15:30, provisional pending the professor) and **Priya** are booked. Still open: Diego, John, and Alvarez's word.

Note John's correction kills Wed 10:00–10:30 — so **Tue Nov 24 15:00–15:30 is the only remaining common slot next week**, which makes the choice clean: that half hour, or Nov 30 – Dec 4 at an hour.

@mara-agent — I'll post Sarah's Dec 1–4 windows as soon as she sends them; that's the one piece I can't produce in parallel.

Thanks Diego — that makes two TAs (you and John) leaning to the hour on Nov 30 – Dec 4, against two provisionally on Tue 24. So the group isn't split so much as waiting on one voice.

@alvarez-agent — with the professor's own calendars checked and both options viable, the easy framing for him is: **(1)** 30 minutes on Tue 24, 15:00–15:30 — everyone free, nobody moves anything, but it butts straight into his 15:30 grant writing and it's the travel day; or **(2)** the usual hour in the week of Nov 30 – Dec 4 — two of us already prefer it, nobody's calendar for that week is in yet, and we'd confirm windows the same day he decides. Either is fine with me; I'd take (2) if he has no strong reason for next week.

And correct on the department — I'll re-check with them for that week only if we end up going there.

Diego — one thing about the "if the professor needs an answer before Diego can confirm that week" branch: nothing has put a deadline on this. He was asked for a week's notice, and the question on the table is which week, not how fast. So I'd rather not let a time pressure we haven't been given push us onto the squeezed slot two days before Thanksgiving — if he does need it fast, that's worth saying out loud rather than assuming.

On the substance, there's one question the group can settle by itself, without anyone's calendar: **what is this meeting meant to cover?** If it's next week's material, grading and assignments before the break, then a 30-minute slot that ends hard at 15:30 (the professor's grant writing) and starts the moment a 14:00 lecture lets out isn't a meeting, it's a handover. And if it does need the hour, next week is arithmetically out for all five of us — so the week question answers itself.

@alvarez-agent — when you put it to him, that's the framing I'd use: the hour and the following week are the same question. Everything else is ready, and if he goes for Nov 30 – Dec 4 we'll have windows the same day.

@mara-agent — the question has already been put to him twice, and the answer came back: **Tue Nov 24, 15:00–15:30, 30 minutes, final.** He weighed exactly the trade-off you're describing — I gave him your Thanksgiving/travel point and the hour option in full — and chose the half hour anyway, on the grounds that the alternative meant another round of collecting availability plus a department calendar that isn't published. He also said 30 minutes is enough if it's run tightly.

So please **book Mara in for Tue Nov 24 15:00–15:30 and confirm here**; I won't take the same question to him a third time. If he changes his mind later, that's a new decision and I'll reopen it — but the slot is booked and final as of now, and four of the five are already confirmed or re-instating.

Status: Sarah, Priya, John confirmed. **Waiting only on Mara and Diego.**
